<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\workshoptitle</span>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">UniReps: 3rd Edition of the Workshop on Unifying Representations in Neural Models</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sébastien Lachapelle
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Samsung AI Lab, Montreal
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">s.lachapelle@samsung.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the identifiability of <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">latent action policy learning</span> (LAPO), a framework introduced recently to discover representations of actions from video data. We formally describe desiderata for such representations, their statistical benefits and potential sources of unidentifiability. Finally, we prove that an entropy-regularized LAPO objective identifies action representations satisfying our desiderata, under suitable conditions. Our analysis provides an explanation for why <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discrete</span> action representations perform well in practice.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction &amp; background</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In robot control, <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">behavior cloning</span> is an approach to learn a policy from action-labeled expert trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. It simply consists in training a policy <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> via supervised learning on data of the form <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},a_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},a_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the action taken by the expert policy in state <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The success of the approach relies on at least two aspects: (i) the demonstrations <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}}_{i},a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}}_{i},a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> have to be generated from a sufficiently good expert policy, and (ii) the number of demonstrations <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> has to be sufficiently large to make learning possible. Although conceptually simple, the framework requires a large amount of action-labeled expert demonstrations to be successful, which can be costly to acquire.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> introduced <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">latent action policy learning</span> (LAPO) which can leverage large corpuses of unannotated video data in order to reduce reliance on action-labeled expert trajectories. LAPO proceeds in three stages. <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, given a large dataset of state/next-state pairs <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\prime})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\prime})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, LAPO minimizes the reconstruction loss</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\mathbb{E}}_{\hat{a}\sim\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\prime})}\lVert{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i}-\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}}_{i},\hat{a})\rVert^{2}_{2}\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\mathbb{E}}_{\hat{a}\sim\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\prime})}\lVert{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i}-\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}}_{i},\hat{a})\rVert^{2}_{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the <span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inverse dynamics model</span> (IDM) <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> encodes pairs <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> into action representations <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> which are then decoded using a <span id="S1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward dynamics model</span> (FDM) <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}^{\prime}=\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}^{\prime}=\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">Secondly</span>, the IDM is used to label the unlabeled video dataset, which yields <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},\hat{a}_{i},{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\prime})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},\hat{a}_{i},{\bm{x}}_{i}^{\prime})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{a}_{i}:=\arg\max_{\hat{a}}\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{a}_{i}:=\arg\max_{\hat{a}}\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>. This dataset is then used to train the <span id="S1.p2.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">latent action policy</span> <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S1.p2.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Thirdly</span>, a learnable head is applied on top of <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> and trained to map the latent actions <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> to actual actions <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> using a much smaller domain-specific action-labeled dataset <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},a_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},a_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{a}&lt;&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&lt;&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{a}&lt;&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>). While doing that, one can choose to either freeze the latent action policy <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> or fine-tune it. The authors showed that, thanks to this approach, fewer action-labeled samples are needed to train a good policy.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          The authors also show <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> can be fine-tuned via reinforcement learning more efficiently.</span></span></span> Since then, this idea has been applied at larger scale in Genie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and augmented with textual goal-conditioning in LAPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the growing importance of this framework, we propose to study the identifiability of LAPO. Although identifiability in representation learning has been the subject of recent research efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, to the best of our knowledge, identifiability in the context of latent action modeling has not been investigated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> First, we postulate a data-generating process for the expert transitions <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a,{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a,{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Data-generating process ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). Next, we provide formal desiderata for the IDM <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to be useful and discuss statistical consequences (<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). We further discuss two potential sources of unidentifiability (<a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Potential sources of unidentifiability ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) and, finally, we prove that an entropy-regularized LAPO objective (<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) is guaranteed to identify an IDM satisfying the said desiderata, under suitable conditions (<a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Identifiability analysis of LAPO</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Data-generating process</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}:=[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}:=[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the current observation, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}:=[0,1]^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}:=[0,1]^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the future observation and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\mathcal{A}}:=\{1,\dots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\mathcal{A}}:=\{1,\dots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a discrete action (in line with practical implementations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>). The most natural situation is when <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to two consecutive frames, i.e. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}:={\bm{x}}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}:={\bm{x}}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{x}}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{x}}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=a^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. But one could also consider different situations where the model is conditioned on a window of past observations: <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{x}}={\bm{x}}^{t-k:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}={\bm{x}}^{t-k:t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a=a^{t-k:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a^{t-k:t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> could correspond to a window of multiple frames in the future.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We assume the current state <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from some (Lebesgue) density function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, an action <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen according to a ground-truth policy <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{x}}\sim p({\bm{x}})\quad\quad\text{and}\quad\quad a\sim\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{x}}\sim p({\bm{x}})\quad\quad\text{and}\quad\quad a\sim\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}},a):=p({\bm{x}})\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}},a):=p({\bm{x}})\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(a):=\int p({\bm{x}},a)d{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a):=\int p({\bm{x}},a)d{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}}\mid a):=p({\bm{x}},a)/p(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}}\mid a):=p({\bm{x}},a)/p(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We assume the future observation <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a deterministic transition model</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a)\text{, where}\ {\bm{g}}:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>, where</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒈</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a)\text{, where}\ {\bm{g}}:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This process induces a joint probability distribution over <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is an abuse of notation since the distribution of <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> has no a density (w.r.t. Lebesgue).</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Support notation.</span> In what follows, the support of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}}\mid a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}}\mid a)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textstyle\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]:=\{{\bm{x}}_{0}\in{\mathcal{X}}\mid\forall\ \text{open neighborhood $U$ of}\ {\bm{x}}_{0},\int_{U}p({\bm{x}}\mid a)d{\bm{x}}&gt;0\}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">∀</mo><mrow><mtext>open neighborhood </mtext><mi>U</mi><mtext> of</mtext></mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>U</mi></msub></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]:=\{{\bm{x}}_{0}\in{\mathcal{X}}\mid\forall\ \text{open neighborhood $U$ of}\ {\bm{x}}_{0},\int_{U}p({\bm{x}}\mid a)d{\bm{x}}&gt;0\}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int d{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int d{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Lebesgue integral. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]</annotation></semantics></math> might depend on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Define also <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]:=\{a\in{\mathcal{A}}\mid p(a)&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]:=\{a\in{\mathcal{A}}\mid p(a)&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]:=\bigcup_{a\in\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]}\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]\times\{a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]:=\bigcup_{a\in\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]}\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]\times\{a\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Desiderata</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formalize three desiderata for an action representation and discuss statistical efficiency. Intuitively, we want to learn an encoder <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> that captures useful information about the ground-truth action <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. To formalize this, we will study</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a):=\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a))\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a):=\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a))\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">defined for all <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]</annotation></semantics></math>. The conditional probability mass function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math> maps pairs <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math> to their corresponding learned action representations <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, potentially in a stochastic way. This is effectively an <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">entanglement map</span>, as studied in identifiable representation learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Our first desideratum is to have a deterministic map from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdesideratum1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_desideratum">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdesideratum1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Desideratum 1</span></span><span id="Thmdesideratum1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Determinism)<span id="Thmdesideratum1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdesideratum1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdesideratum1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdesideratum1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a function <math id="Thmdesideratum1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdesideratum1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)={\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)={\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a))}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdesideratum1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdesideratum1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice how the learned action representation <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> might depend on the current state <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a dependence is undesirable since it signifies that the meaning of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. how it relates to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, depends on the current state <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We illustrate this unfortunate state of affairs with a simple example.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmexample1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a manipulation task where <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}:=\{\texttt{left},\texttt{right}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">left</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">right</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}:=\{\texttt{left},\texttt{right}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{0},a=\texttt{left}),\quad 2={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{0},a=\texttt{right})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">left</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">right</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{0},a=\texttt{left}),\quad 2={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{0},a=\texttt{right}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{1},a=\texttt{left}),\quad 1={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{1},a=\texttt{right})\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">left</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">right</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{1},a=\texttt{left}),\quad 1={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}}_{1},a=\texttt{right})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We can see that the meaning of <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the context <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>: When in state <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\texttt{left}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">left</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\texttt{left}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas in state <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\texttt{right}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">right</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\texttt{right}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This undesirable phenomenon, described informally by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Section 6.2]</cite>, can be thought of as a form of entanglement since <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> entangles both <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This motivates:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdesideratum2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_desideratum">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdesideratum2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Desideratum 2</span></span><span id="Thmdesideratum2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Disentanglement)<span id="Thmdesideratum2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdesideratum2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdesideratum2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdesideratum2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a function <math id="Thmdesideratum2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for all <math id="Thmdesideratum2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdesideratum2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a)={\bm{v}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a)={\bm{v}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we want the latent action <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> to reveal all there is to know about the ground-truth action <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. More formally, we want that two distinct actions <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> never map to the same latent action <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdesideratum3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_desideratum">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdesideratum3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Desideratum 3</span></span><span id="Thmdesideratum3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informativeness)<span id="Thmdesideratum3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdesideratum3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdesideratum3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdesideratum3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The function <math id="Thmdesideratum3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical efficiency.</span> As explained in <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction &amp; background ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an encoder/IDM <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to label the action-free video dataset, yielding <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},\hat{a}_{i},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i})\}^{N}_{i=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},\hat{a}_{i},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i})\}^{N}_{i=1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the IDM satisfies our desiderata, the newly labeled dataset is actually <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{v}}(a_{i}),{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i})\}^{N}_{i=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{v}}(a_{i}),{\bm{x}}^{\prime}_{i})\}^{N}_{i=1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the action taken by the expert policy <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the latent action policy <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> trained on this data will approximate the distribution of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\sim\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sim\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that there exists a transformation <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> (any extension of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}^{-1}:{\bm{v}}(\textnormal{supp}[p(a)])\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}^{-1}:{\bm{v}}(\textnormal{supp}[p(a)])\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) such that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\hat{a})\sim\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\hat{a})\sim\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}\sim\pi(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}\sim\pi(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, to get the expert policy <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, we only need to learn <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> “on top of” the latent action policy <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> using the smaller action-labeled dataset. Had <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> been dependent on <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such a transformation <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> would not exist, forcing us to resort to either fine-tuning <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> or learning a map <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\times\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\times\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> on top of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, both of which are expected to be less statistically efficient than learning the simpler function <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now present a formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective. <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> will show that, under suitable assumptions, its solutions must satisfy the desiderata of <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to learn a deterministic encoder, we add an entropy regularizer <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\hat{q}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})):={-{\mathbb{E}}_{\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}\log\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\hat{q}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})):={-{\mathbb{E}}_{\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}\log\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the limit of infinite data, our entropy-regularized LAPO objective is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\hat{\bm{g}}\in{\mathcal{G}},\hat{q}\in{\mathcal{Q}}}{\mathbb{E}}_{p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}\left[{\mathbb{E}}_{\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}\lVert{\bm{x}}^{\prime}-\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})\rVert^{2}_{2}+\beta H(\hat{q}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}))\right]\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\hat{\bm{g}}\in{\mathcal{G}},\hat{q}\in{\mathcal{Q}}}{\mathbb{E}}_{p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}\left[{\mathbb{E}}_{\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})}\lVert{\bm{x}}^{\prime}-\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})\rVert^{2}_{2}+\beta H(\hat{q}(\cdot\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}))\right]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls regularization, and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively the hypothesis spaces for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(FDM hypothesis space <math id="Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A function <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{g}}:{\mathcal{X}}\times\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{g}}:{\mathcal{X}}\times\hat{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if, for all <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}\in\hat{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}\in\hat{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous in <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(IDM hypothesis space <math id="Thmdefinition2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{A}}:=\{1,\dots,\hat{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{A}}:=\{1,\dots,\hat{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of action representations <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. A function <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\times{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}:\hat{\mathcal{A}}\times{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if (i) for all <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})\in{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})\in{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sum_{\hat{a}\in\hat{\mathcal{A}}}\ \hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sum_{\hat{a}\in\hat{\mathcal{A}}}\ \hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})=1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) for all <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}\in\hat{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}\in\hat{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous in <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that our identifiability guarantee, <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, does not assume <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=k</annotation></semantics></math>, only <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}\geq k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One can easily see that both terms in Problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are lower bounded by zero. Additionally, <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ Appendix ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (in appendix) shows that there exists <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{g}}^{*},q^{*})\in{\mathcal{G}}\times{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{g}}^{*},q^{*})\in{\mathcal{G}}\times{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that both terms are equal to zero (under <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Continuous 𝒈). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Injectivity). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This means that, at optimality, the entropy regularizer must be equal to zero thus forcing the learned IDM <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to be deterministic for all <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})]</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, at optimality, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})=\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}=\hat{\bm{f}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})=\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}=\hat{\bm{f}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> for some function <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{f}}:\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{f}}:\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The above development begs the question: Why are we considering a stochastic IDM <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}</annotation></semantics></math> to later regularize it to be deterministic? A perhaps more natural route would be to directly train a deterministic encoder <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{f}}:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{f}}:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>. From an optimization perspective, a stochastic encoder is helpful as it unlocks gradient computation via the reparameterization trick <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. From a theoretical perspective, the continuity condition on <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is crucial for our proof, as it excludes pathological encoders <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> that would present “jumps” on the connected components of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})]</annotation></semantics></math>. We conjecture that the VQ-VAE approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which is limited to deterministic discrete encoders, can in principle lead to such pathological behaviors. We leave this for future work.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Potential sources of unidentifiability</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that, without assumptions on the data-generating process or without restrictions on the hypothesis classes <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> admits degenerate solutions which do not satisfy our desiderata of <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2</span></span><span id="Thmexample2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(No restriction on <math id="Thmexample2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmexample2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In principle, one can choose <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{A}}:={\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{A}}:={\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}):=\delta(\hat{a}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}):=\delta(\hat{a}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the Dirac function. Hence the IDM outputs <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> deterministically. By choosing <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})=\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a})=\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, we clearly solve Problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, but the action representation <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is uninteresting. In fact, this can be understood as a violation of <a href="#Thmdesideratum2" title="Desideratum 2 (Disentanglement). ‣ 2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Desideratum</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> since <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}={\bm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}={\bm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> clearly depends on <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3</span></span><span id="Thmexample3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deterministic <math id="Thmexample3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmexample3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="Thmexample3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})=\mathbf{1}(a=\pi({\bm{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a\mid{\bm{x}})=\mathbf{1}(a=\pi({\bm{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the ground-truth policy is deterministic. In that case, one can solve the reconstruction problem by choosing <math id="Thmexample3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a}):={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\pi({\bm{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\hat{a}):={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\pi({\bm{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="Thmexample3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\pi({\bm{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}^{\prime}={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},\pi({\bm{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math> with probability one. In that case, the latent action <math id="Thmexample3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely ignored by the FDM and thus the IDM <math id="Thmexample3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> could simply output the same action deterministically, which would clearly present a violation of <a href="#Thmdesideratum3" title="Desideratum 3 (Informativeness). ‣ 2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Desideratum</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Main identifiability result</h3>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01337v1/assum_3_4.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/78;" width="476" height="78"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Assume <math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}:=\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}:=\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide sufficient conditions on the data-generating process under which the solutions of Problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are guaranteed to satisfy the desiderata of <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First of all, we require the ground-truth FDM to be continuous.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Continuous <math id="Thmassumption1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒈</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the ground-truth FDM <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous in <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we require that different actions always have different effects in the data-generating process. We formalize this as a form of injectivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Injectivity)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}\in{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}\in{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\not=a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\not=a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a_{1})\not={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a_{1})\not={\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The last two assumptions put topological restrictions on the support of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that a set <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">connected</span> if it “holds in one piece” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. See <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an illustration.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a\in\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a\in\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a)]</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected subset of <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all pairs <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}\in\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}\in\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a_{1})]\cap\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a_{2})]\not=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a_{1})]\cap\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}}\mid a_{2})]\not=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> are both satisfied for example if <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]={\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]={\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to state the main identifiability result of this work. It shows that, under the assumptions introduced above, the encoder/IDM <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> learned by optimizing Problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> must satisfy the desiderata of <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Its proof can be found in the appendix. Recall <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a):=\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a):=\hat{q}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},{\bm{g}}({\bm{x}},a))}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}\geq k</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\bm{g}},\hat{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\bm{g}},\hat{q})</annotation></semantics></math> be a solution<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">3</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Under </span><a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Continuous 𝒈). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="footnote3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> and </span><a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Injectivity). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="footnote3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, a solution is guaranteed to exist by </span><a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ Appendix ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="footnote3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> in appendix.</span></span></span></span> of Problem <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with hypothesis classes <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (FDM hypothesis space 𝒢). ‣ 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (IDM hypothesis space 𝒬). ‣ 2.3 A formal entropy-regularized LAPO objective ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Continuous 𝒈). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Injectivity). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> hold, then </span><a href="#Thmdesideratum1" title="Desideratum 1 (Determinism). ‣ 2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Desideratum</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds, i.e. there exists a function </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that, for all </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)=\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a))\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)=\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}={\bm{v}}({\bm{x}},a))\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Continuous 𝒈). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Injectivity). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> hold, then </span><a href="#Thmdesideratum2" title="Desideratum 2 (Disentanglement). ‣ 2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Desideratum</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds, i.e. there exists a mapping </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that, for all </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},a)\in\textnormal{supp}[p({\bm{x}},a)]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)=\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}={\bm{v}}(a))\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}(\hat{a}\mid{\bm{x}},a)=\mathbf{1}(\hat{a}={\bm{v}}(a))\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Continuous 𝒈). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumptions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Injectivity). ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 2.5 Main identifiability result ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> hold, then </span><a href="#Thmdesideratum3" title="Desideratum 3 (Informativeness). ‣ 2.2 Desiderata ‣ 2 Identifiability analysis of LAPO ‣ On the Identifiability of Latent Action Policies" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Desideratum</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds, i.e. the mapping </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bm{v}}:\textnormal{supp}[p(a)]\rightarrow\hat{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defined above is injective.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
